  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA pre-frontal trough moving across NY and a low pressure system\nmoving up the east coast are the main weather features impacting\nthe area today. These features are generating some radar returns\nover the area, but with dry air at the surface, most of the\nprecipitation will not make it to the ground. With varying cloud\ncover over the area, temperatures will range from the mid 50s to\nupper 60s across the area, with higher temperatures expected\nacross the northern Finger Lakes and Mohawk Valley. \n\nA cold front will push through tonight as a low pressure system\nslides across Ontario. Scattered showers will be possible \nacross CNY starting after midnight, moving from west to east.\nCold air will filter in behind the front, allowing lake effect\nshowers to develop across portions of Onondaga, Madison and S\nOneida counties. These showers should linger into the late\nmorning/early afternoon before a surface high moving in from the\nwest ends the precipitation chances. Early morning temps in the\nmid 40s to low 50s will climb to the mid to upper 50s by the\nafternoon hours.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \n\nHigh pressure will dominate the weather pattern for most of the\nweek. Some of the coldest air of the season will get dragged\ninto the region Monday and Tuesday. Overnight lows will fall\ninto the mid to upper 30s Monday night and Tuesday night. \nPatchy frost will be possible, especially where winds go calm.\nThe center of the high is now forecast to move overhead a little\nlater, which would keep winds active a little longer and limit \nfrost formation. If the high can move eastward a little quicker,\nwe will have more widespread frost and may even have some\nsheltered valleys hit freezing. Cool temps stick around during\nthe day on Tuesday, with 50s forecast across much of the area.\nTuesday night may be cold again, but the speed of the ridge\naxis and surface high moving eastward will determine if we are\nin the low to mid 30s or mid 30s to low 40s. The latest model\nruns have sped up the ridge and thus the WAA. We will keep an\neye on this. Warm air will return for the end of the week, with\n60s for highs and 40s for lows expected. A passing weak wave \nmay kick off some scattered rain showers across the northern \nportion of the CWA on Thursday, but most should stay dry through\nthe end of the week.",
